on the completion of my own physics  degree I would like to enlighten others  on what content you can expect to be  involved when studying physics you will  start off with broad courses with titles  that cover most of the areas you will  spend the next years going into more  depth about these areas include  introductory mechanics waves  thermodynamics electromagnetism the  special theory of relativity and the  quantum theory of matter to pass these  courses you will need to understand how  to pull information out of a question  and plug it into a formula you  conveniently spotted on a formula sheet  practice and understanding helps but  there is a certain distance you can  freestyle with educated guesses and a  process of elimination you need enough  algebra and trigonometry to be able to  rearrange these equations to get any  variable you want the next year  relativity becomes calculating the  dynamics of particles traveling near the  speed of light and getting your head  around space-time transformations and  different reference friends quantum  theory becomes solving the Schrodinger  equation waves become optical matrices  mechanics is supposed to be  understanding how a gyroscope works but  I still don't think I do  electromagnetism is the connection that  electrons move at relativistic speeds  and people will keep mentioning Fourier  series  you will write long lab reports for  experiments that almost make sense and  that will either bring you and your  classmates together or drive you apart  in New Zealand a physics degree is three  years and in my third year I was exposed  to the world of lattices that make up  materials there are equations to predict  gases and particles with lots of names  there are two big pillar courses that  were both tough and rewarding classical  mechanics and advanced quantum mechanics  classical mechanics looks at everyday  objects and translates them into the  language of lagrangian's and  Hamiltonians where energy and symmetries  can reveal solutions to complex problems  it touches on a new description of  special relativity with tensors quantum  mechanics starts to be written and  directed and you talk more about  commutations observables and spin you  will usually have the chance to do your  own research project for a semester if  you are lucky thus we'll be working with  a supervisor on a real problem in  physics and applying some of what you've  learned and some of your own ideas to  doing real research if you are unlucky  you'll be given some fake problem that  student toil over every year and write a  mess of code in the process  just enrolling in a course and existing  in the lectures won't get you a physics  degree you actually have to learn the  content by third year you will be given  less and less resources and expected  more to derive anything you can't  remember at many times it will seem like  there is too much to be done and that it  is impossible to learn enough of it you  certainly need to be able to apply a bit  of minimum effort maximum gain recognize  what needs to be done and don't toil  over details that aren't important much  of the learning process seemed  inefficient but you need to work out how  to work the most efficiently in that  environment  I have graduated from my degree feeling  like I know very little physics you are  exposed to many different areas and the  problems I were given and had time to  work on were only a subset of what could  have been done going further you can  choose to specialize in one or more  areas but go on other days where one  could be a true expert on all of physics  I'm happy to answer any questions from  people wanting to go down the same path  and good luck with your studies
 

